{
  "abstract": "Finite quadratic interpolation determinants require lower bounds for a local lattice index with pure Hasse prices $p^{\\lfloor j/p\\rfloor}$. The all-order index and a positive cubic excess bound are known, but collective congruences at depths proportional to the source size are harder to retain. We construct such congruences on the original support $r+Ks<R$ for every prime $p$ and integer $p<K\\le p^3$. An exact second Frobenius-ghost relation makes approximate-center powers inexpensive in a valuation-weighted filtration. Truncating their coefficients gives primitive witnesses with support growing linearly in precision. A second filtration lowers bounded ghost-digit weights: endpoint support and normalized denominators control every higher carry, while exact digit maxima recover original support with logarithmic loss. A more accurate conjugate center can reduce the jet-error cost at the same scalar price. Retaining the witnesses' leading labels then combines independent directions by their union, with overlaps counted once. This gives an explicit cubic coefficient from the areas of two regions and their intersection. At $p=3,K=17$, it improves the published coefficient $1/13872$ to $3755/26030808$. For $(R,J)=(28560,3769)$, the complete union gives excess $50\\,229\\,653$ and total index at least $22\\,963\\,131\\,739$. Exact carry expansions, independent label counts, and original-node Mahler certificates accompany the proofs, including failing controls. A replay improves all 19 established positive physical determinant contracts while preserving rank, nodes, support, derivative prices, and metric estimate; both negative controls remain negative. The argument extends an earlier finite-model filtration to the actual linked-node algebra. Optimality and the exact interior profile remain open. No new discriminant endpoint, census, or classification is claimed.",
